<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Windows, because the library already is. It shells to winget, writes to
      %APPDATA%, reasons about drive letters, and now reads the App Paths key -
      which is how Windows itself answers "where is vlc.exe" for a program that
      registered properly and never touched PATH. Claiming to be portable was
      a fiction that cost a real bug.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PyreMedia.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The only two packages this library depends on, and neither was added lightly -
    everything else here is written against the framework alone.

    The Grand Comics Database publishes its data as a SQLite file and nothing
    else: no API, no export in another format. Reading it means speaking SQLite,
    and hand-rolling that would be far worse than taking Microsoft's own
    provider. It brings a small native library per platform, which is the price.

    SharpCompress reads RAR, which .NET does not and never will - RAR's format is
    documented but its compression is proprietary enough that no framework ships
    a decoder. It matters because .cbr is RAR: measured against a real library,
    8,342 of 37,425 comics were .cbr and every one of them was invisible without
    this. MIT licensed, pure managed, no native dependency.

    Pinned at 0.48.0 or later on purpose. Everything before it carries
    GHSA-6c8g-7p36-r338, a path-traversal in WriteToDirectory - which this
    program would never call, since it reads entries into memory and never
    extracts to disk, but taking the patched version costs nothing.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="SharpCompress" Version="0.48.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
